## 1. Synchronous JavaScript

Synchronous code executes one statement at a time, in order.

```js
console.log("First");
console.log("Second");
console.log("Third");
```

**Output:**

```text
First
Second
Third
```

**What I learned:** JavaScript normally executes synchronous statements in sequence.

## 2. Asynchronous JavaScript

Asynchronous programming allows JavaScript to continue working while waiting for operations such as API requests.

```js
console.log("Start");

setTimeout(function () {
    console.log("After waiting");
}, 1000);

console.log("End");
```

**Output:**

```text
Start
End
After waiting
```

**What I learned:** Asynchronous operations help applications handle waiting without blocking the main flow of JavaScript.

## 3. Callbacks

A callback is a function passed to another function to be called later.

```js
function greet(name, callback) {
    console.log("Hello " + name);
    callback();
}

function finished() {
    console.log("Greeting completed");
}

greet("Ali", finished);
```

**Output:**

```text
Hello Ali
Greeting completed
```

**What I learned:** Callbacks are useful for handling events and completing operations in sequence.

## 4. Promises

A Promise represents the eventual success or failure of an asynchronous operation.

```js
const result = new Promise(function (resolve, reject) {
    const success = true;

    if (success) {
        resolve("Operation successful");
    } else {
        reject("Operation failed");
    }
});

result.then(function (value) {
    console.log(value);
});
```

**What I learned:** Promises provide a structured way to handle asynchronous results.

## 5. `.then()` and `.catch()`

- `.then()` handles a successful result.
- `.catch()` handles errors in the Promise chain.

```js
fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1")
    .then(function (response) {
        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("Request failed");
        }

        return response.json();
    })
    .then(function (data) {
        console.log(data);
    })
    .catch(function (error) {
        console.error("Error:", error);
    });
```

**What I learned:** How to process API responses and handle failures using Promise chains.

## 6. Async and Await

`async` and `await` provide a readable way to work with Promises.

```js
async function getUser() {
    const response = await fetch(
        "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1"
    );

    if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error("Request failed");
    }

    const data = await response.json();

    console.log(data);
}

getUser().catch(console.error);
```

**What I learned:** `await` waits for a Promise to settle inside an async function before continuing to the next statement.

## 7. Try...Catch

`try...catch` handles errors so an application can respond instead of failing silently.

```js
async function loadData() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
            "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1"
        );

        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("Unable to load data");
        }

        const data = await response.json();
        console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Something went wrong:", error.message);
    }
}

loadData();
```

**What I learned:** Error handling is important when network requests fail or data processing encounters a problem.

## 8. ES Modules — Import and Export

Modules allow JavaScript code to be separated into reusable files.

**math.js**

```js
export function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

**app.js**

```js
import { add } from "./math.js";

console.log(add(5, 3));
```

**HTML**

```html
<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>
```

**Output:**

```text
8
```

**What I learned:** Export reusable functions and import them where they are needed.

## 9. DOM Manipulation and Events

The DOM allows JavaScript to read and update HTML elements.

```html
<p id="message">Hello</p>
<button id="changeButton">Change Text</button>
```

```js
const message = document.getElementById("message");
const button = document.getElementById("changeButton");

button.addEventListener("click", function () {
    message.textContent = "Text changed!";
});
```

**What I learned:** How to select elements, listen for events, and update page content dynamically.

### Preventing Form Reload

```js
form.addEventListener("submit", function (event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    console.log("Form submitted without reloading");
});
```

`event.preventDefault()` stops the browser's default form submission behavior.

## 10. Classes and `this`

Classes provide a way to create objects with shared properties and methods.

```js
class Student {
    constructor(name, marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    showResult() {
        console.log(this.name + " scored " + this.marks);
    }
}

const student = new Student("Ali", 90);
student.showResult();
```

**Output:**

```text
Ali scored 90
```

**What I learned:**

- `class` defines a class.
- `constructor()` initializes an object.
- `this` refers to the object associated with the method call in this example.

## 11. Debugging with Browser DevTools

Browser DevTools helps identify errors and inspect application behavior.

```js
const temperature = 30;

console.log("Temperature:", temperature);
console.error("Example error");
console.table([
    { city: "Lahore", temperature: 30 },
    { city: "Karachi", temperature: 32 }
]);
```

**Debugging steps:**

1. Open the website in the browser.
2. Press `F12`.
3. Open the Console tab.
4. Read errors and inspect logged values.
5. Use the Sources tab and breakpoints to pause JavaScript execution and inspect variables.

**What I learned:** How to use console messages and breakpoints to investigate bugs.

## 12. Fetch API and JSON

The Fetch API makes HTTP requests. JSON is a common format for exchanging structured data.

```js
fetch("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users")
    .then(function (response) {
        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("HTTP request failed");
        }

        return response.json();
    })
    .then(function (users) {
        users.forEach(function (user) {
            console.log(user.name);
        });
    })
    .catch(function (error) {
        console.error(error);
    });
```

**What I learned:**

- Send HTTP requests with `fetch()`.
- Convert a response body to JavaScript data using `response.json()`.
- Handle HTTP errors and network failures.
- Render API data dynamically.

