```

## Key Code from My Project

### 1. Validate the City Input

```js
const cityInputValue = cityInput.value.trim();

if (cityInputValue === "") {
    message.textContent = "Please enter a city name!";
    return;
}
```

This prevents an empty search from triggering an API request.

### 2. Convert the City Name for a URL

```js
const encodedCity = encodeURIComponent(cityInputValue);

const geoUrl =
    `https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search?name=${encodedCity}&count=1`;
```

This safely encodes city names before including them in the URL.

### 3. Fetch Weather Data

```js
fetch(geoUrl)
    .then(function (response) {
        if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error("Unable to find city.");
        }

        return response.json();
    })
    .then(function (data) {
        console.log(data);
    })
    .catch(function (error) {
        console.error("Error:", error);
    });
```

This demonstrates fetching data, converting JSON, and handling errors.

### 4. Convert Weather Codes into Conditions and Icons

```js
function WeatherCondition(weatherCode) {
    let condition = "Unknown conditions";
    let icon = "🌡️";

    if (weatherCode === 0) {
        condition = "Clear sky";
        icon = "☀️";
    } else if (weatherCode === 1) {
        condition = "Mainly clear";
        icon = "🌤️";
    } else if (weatherCode === 2) {
        condition = "Partly cloudy";
        icon = "⛅";
    } else if (weatherCode === 3) {
        condition = "Overcast";
        icon = "☁️";
    }

    return {
        condition: condition,
        icon: icon
    };
}
```

The complete project function also handles fog, drizzle, rain, snow, and thunderstorms.

### 5. Generate Forecast Cards Dynamically

```js
forecastGrid.innerHTML = "";

for (let i = 0; i < daily.time.length; i++) {
    const card = document.createElement("article");
    card.className = "forecast-card";

    card.innerHTML = `
        <p class="forecast-day">${daily.time[i]}</p>
        <p class="forecast-temp">
            ${daily.temperature_2m_max[i]}°
            <span>${daily.temperature_2m_min[i]}°</span>
        </p>
    `;

    forecastGrid.appendChild(card);
}
```

This creates a card for each forecast day using data received from the API.

## How to Run the Project

1. Clone the repository:

   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/mhdches/mern-8-week-roadmap.git
   ```

2. Open the `weather-app-week2` folder in VS Code.

3. Run `index.html` using Live Server or open it in a browser.

4. Enter a city name and check the weather.

